Article 2 For the purposes of this Law, "biosecurity" means the ability of the State to effectively prevent and respond to threats from dangerous biological factors and related elements, the stable and healthy development of biotechnology, the state in which people's life and health as well as ecosystems are relatively free from danger and threats, and the capacity of the biological field to maintain national security and sustainable development.
This Law shall apply to the following activities:
(1) prevention and control of major emerging or sudden infectious diseases, animal and plant epidemics;
(2) research, development, and application of biotechnology;
(3) biosecurity management of pathogenic microorganism laboratories;
(4) security management of human genetic resources and biological resources;
(5) prevention of invasion by alien species and protection of biodiversity;
(6) response to antimicrobial resistance;
(7) prevention of bioterrorism attacks and defense against threats of biological weapons; and
(8) other activities related to biosecurity.

Article 15 The State shall establish a biosecurity risk investigation and assessment system. The national biosecurity work coordination mechanism shall regularly organize biosecurity risk investigations and assessments based on data, information, and other materials from risk monitoring.
In any of the following circumstances, the relevant departments shall promptly conduct biosecurity risk investigations and assessments, and take necessary risk prevention and control measures according to law:
(1) Where a possible biosecurity risk is discovered through risk monitoring or upon receiving a report;
(2) Where it is necessary to determine key areas and key projects for supervision and management, or to formulate or adjust biosecurity-related catalogs or lists;
(3) Where a major emerging or sudden infectious disease, animal or plant epidemic, or other event endangering biosecurity occurs; and
(4) Other circumstances requiring investigation and assessment.

Article 26 When conducting biosecurity supervision and inspection, relevant departments of people's governments at or above the county level may take the following measures according to law:
(1) Enter the entity or location under inspection, or the place suspected of committing biosecurity violations, for on-site monitoring, survey, inspection, or verification;
(2) Inquire about the situation from relevant entities and individuals;
(3) Review and copy relevant documents, materials, archives, records, vouchers, etc.;
(4) Seal up places and facilities suspected of being used for biosecurity violations;
(5) Seize tools, equipment, and related articles suspected of being used for biosecurity violations; and
(6) Other measures provided for by laws and regulations.
The biosecurity violation information of relevant entities and individuals shall be incorporated into the national credit information sharing platform according to law.

Article 56 The following activities shall be subject to the approval of the competent health department of the State Council:
(1) collecting important genetic pedigrees or human genetic resources of specific regions within China, or collecting human genetic resources of types or quantities specified by the competent health department of the State Council;
(2) preserving human genetic resources within China;
(3) conducting international scientific research cooperation using human genetic resources within China;
(4) transporting, mailing, or carrying human genetic resource materials out of China.
The provisions of the preceding paragraph shall not include the collection, preservation, and related activities of human genetic resources for purposes such as clinical diagnosis and treatment, blood collection and supply services, investigating and handling illegal or criminal activities, doping testing, and funeral services.
To obtain market authorization for relevant drugs and medical devices in China, conducting international cooperative clinical trials using human genetic resources within China at clinical trial institutions, without the export of human genetic resources, shall not require approval; however, before conducting the clinical trial, the types, quantities, and purposes of the human genetic resources to be used shall be filed with the competent health department of the State Council.
Foreign organizations, individuals, and institutions established or actually controlled by them shall not collect or preserve human genetic resources within China, and shall not provide human genetic resources within China to overseas parties.

Article 66 The State shall formulate a development plan for biosafety undertakings, strengthen biosafety capacity building, and improve the capability and level of responding to biosafety incidents.
The people's governments at or above the county level shall support the development of biosafety undertakings, and in accordance with the division of powers, shall include in the government budget the relevant expenditures for supporting the development of the following biosafety undertakings:
(1) construction and operation of monitoring networks;
(2) reserve of materials for emergency response and prevention and control;
(3) construction and operation of key infrastructure;
(4) research and development of key technologies and products;
(5) survey and preservation of human genetic resources and biological resources;
(6) other important biosafety undertakings as provided for by laws and regulations.

Article 78 Where a person, in violation of the provisions of this Law, commits any of the following acts, the relevant departments of the people's government at or above the county level shall, according to their respective responsibilities, order correction, confiscate the illegal gains, give a warning, and may concurrently impose a fine of not less than 100,000 yuan but not more than one million yuan:
(1) purchasing or introducing important equipment or special biological agents on the control list without registration or without reporting to the relevant department of the State Council for record;
(2) an individual purchasing or possessing important equipment or special biological agents on the control list;
(3) an individual establishing a pathogenic microorganism laboratory or engaging in pathogenic microorganism experimental activities;
(4) entering a high-level pathogenic microorganism laboratory without the approval of the laboratory director.
